WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.290
It is hearing night in Torrance.

2
00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:06.066
The item is a contested redevelopment proposal.

3
00:00:06.066 --> 00:00:12.268
The packet includes technical studies, revised conditions, environmental review, and late correspondence.

4
00:00:12.268 --> 00:00:14.926
One commissioner received an outside contact.

5
00:00:14.926 --> 00:00:17.879
The applicant disputes a new staff recommendation.

6
00:00:17.879 --> 00:00:21.010
Residents raise equity, traffic, and health concerns.

7
00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.668
Your task is not to produce a unanimous room.

8
00:00:23.668 --> 00:00:26.680
It is to run a lawful, fair, intelligible decision.

9
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:32.350
This capstone is study preparation, not legal advice and not a script for a pending application.

10
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:37.253
Procedures, deadlines, findings, and state-law constraints depend on current facts.

11
00:00:37.253 --> 00:00:48.121
Before any real Torrance hearing, use the posted agenda, current staff report, governing code, adopted procedures, and real-time advice from the chair, clerk, staff, and city attorney.

12
00:00:48.121 --> 00:00:50.671
Preparation begins with a decision charter.

13
00:00:50.671 --> 00:01:01.583
In one paragraph, identify the decision-making body, action requested, final or recommending role, legal authority, required findings, environmental action, appeal path, and deadlines.

14
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:05.675
If any element is uncertain, resolve it before discussing the merits.

15
00:01:05.675 --> 00:01:07.454
Next build a standards matrix.

16
00:01:07.454 --> 00:01:10.597
Put each finding and policy criterion in its own row.

17
00:01:10.597 --> 00:01:16.883
Add supporting evidence, contrary evidence, source quality, proposed conditions, and unanswered questions.

18
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:20.857
Separate mandatory objective standards from discretionary criteria.

19
00:01:20.857 --> 00:01:23.822
Separate CEQA questions from entitlement findings.

20
00:01:23.822 --> 00:01:27.440
Separate project impacts from broader policy dissatisfaction.

21
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:28.804
Then audit the process.

22
00:01:28.804 --> 00:01:30.049
Was notice completed?

23
00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:33.133
Are agenda materials publicly available as required?

24
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.030
Were late documents distributed?

25
00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.936
Are language and disability accommodations ready?

26
00:01:37.936 --> 00:01:39.656
Are speaker procedures clear?

27
00:01:39.656 --> 00:01:46.535
Have ex parte contacts, site visits, gifts, financial interests, and potential bias issues been raised with counsel?

28
00:01:46.535 --> 00:01:50.153
Does a continuance affect a statutory clock or hearing limit?

29
00:01:50.153 --> 00:01:53.005
Meet with yourself before meeting with anyone else.

30
00:01:53.005 --> 00:01:58.261
Write the strongest lawful argument for approval and the strongest lawful argument for denial.

31
00:01:58.261 --> 00:02:01.336
Identify which fact would change your preliminary view.

32
00:02:01.336 --> 00:02:03.573
This exercise detects confirmation bias.

33
00:02:03.573 --> 00:02:12.016
Do not circulate private deliberation among a quorum, and do not use serial communications to develop collective concurrence outside a noticed meeting.

34
00:02:12.016 --> 00:02:15.650
Send technical questions through authorized staff channels early.

35
00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.508
Ask for page citations and written answers that can enter the record.

36
00:02:19.508 --> 00:02:23.367
Do not direct staff to manufacture support for your preferred result.

37
00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:27.001
Ask neutral questions: Which finding does this condition support?

38
00:02:27.001 --> 00:02:29.294
Where is the model assumption documented?

39
00:02:29.294 --> 00:02:31.418
Does state law permit this discretion?

40
00:02:31.418 --> 00:02:33.879
What evidence addresses the contrary report?

41
00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:37.569
The chair should open the item by naming the action and procedure.

42
00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:39.861
Obtain required disclosures and recusals.

43
00:02:39.861 --> 00:02:45.677
Ask staff to confirm notice, jurisdiction, environmental posture, decision deadline, and recommendation.

44
00:02:45.677 --> 00:02:49.702
If there is a threshold defect, resolve it before a long merits hearing.

45
00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.995
A crowd's time is also a public resource.

46
00:02:51.995 --> 00:02:55.337
Staff's presentation should orient, not recite the packet.

47
00:02:55.337 --> 00:03:02.714
It should describe the proposal, governing standards, key evidence, material changes, unresolved issues, and recommended action.

48
00:03:02.714 --> 00:03:08.131
Commissioners should ask clarifying questions without arguing a position through the question.

49
00:03:08.131 --> 00:03:13.952
The applicant or appellant should receive the presentation opportunity provided by current procedure.

50
00:03:13.952 --> 00:03:18.043
Questions should test evidence, authority, feasibility, and conditions.

51
00:03:18.043 --> 00:03:21.616
Before public comment, explain what remains open for decision.

52
00:03:21.616 --> 00:03:24.498
If density is mandated by controlling law, say so.

53
00:03:24.498 --> 00:03:28.589
If design, operations, or findings remain discretionary, identify them.

54
00:03:28.589 --> 00:03:34.294
Explain time limits, translation or accommodation procedures, and how written comments are treated.

55
00:03:34.294 --> 00:03:39.654
Consistent structure reduces conflict because people understand where their information fits.

56
00:03:39.654 --> 00:03:41.440
Listen for evidence and values.

57
00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.529
A resident may report repeated flooding, identify a missing receptor in a noise study, or express a general fear of change.

58
00:03:48.529 --> 00:03:52.909
All deserve respectful treatment, but they serve different analytical roles.

59
00:03:52.909 --> 00:03:55.617
Ask concise follow-up questions when necessary.

60
00:03:55.617 --> 00:03:57.922
Do not cross-examine or debate speakers.

61
00:03:57.922 --> 00:04:00.746
Do not let applause determine evidentiary weight.

62
00:04:00.746 --> 00:04:02.285
Manage late claims openly.

63
00:04:02.285 --> 00:04:04.121
Name the document or assertion.

64
00:04:04.121 --> 00:04:06.548
Ask staff whether it is new and material.

65
00:04:06.548 --> 00:04:11.225
Ask counsel whether it may be considered and whether response time is required.

66
00:04:11.225 --> 00:04:16.199
If a continuance is proposed, state the missing information and timing consequences.

67
00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:21.527
If the hearing proceeds, explain why participants had a meaningful opportunity to respond.

68
00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:26.501
When public testimony ends, decide clearly whether to close or continue the hearing.

69
00:04:26.501 --> 00:04:36.802
A closed hearing does not prevent commissioners from asking staff legal or record-based questions, but materially new evidence may require reopening or another fairness step.

70
00:04:36.802 --> 00:04:38.875
Follow local procedure and counsel.

71
00:04:38.875 --> 00:04:42.605
Never use closure to shield a decisive new claim from response.

72
00:04:42.605 --> 00:04:45.387
Deliberation should proceed finding by finding.

73
00:04:45.387 --> 00:04:47.163
Begin with areas of agreement.

74
00:04:47.163 --> 00:04:48.762
Identify disputed evidence.

75
00:04:48.762 --> 00:04:51.485
State which source is more persuasive and why.

76
00:04:51.485 --> 00:04:53.735
Address significant contrary evidence.

77
00:04:53.735 --> 00:04:54.979
Then test conditions.

78
00:04:54.979 --> 00:05:01.373
Is each authorized, connected to the concern, precise, enforceable, timed, and assigned to an administrator?

79
00:05:01.373 --> 00:05:05.754
Remove conditions that are symbolic, duplicative, or impossible to verify.

80
00:05:05.754 --> 00:05:07.606
The motion is the legal product.

81
00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:08.937
State the exact action.

82
00:05:08.937 --> 00:05:11.020
Identify plan and document versions.

83
00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.782
Take the environmental action in the sequence advised by counsel.

84
00:05:14.782 --> 00:05:16.750
Adopt, modify, or reject findings.

85
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.801
Read substantive condition changes into the record with exact wording.

86
00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:25.084
State effective dates, monitoring duties, and appeal rights when required.

87
00:05:25.084 --> 00:05:29.829
If the maker and seconder disagree about an amendment, resolve that before voting.

88
00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.855
After the vote, the work continues.

89
00:05:31.855 --> 00:05:36.774
Staff must prepare the final resolution or recommendation consistent with the motion.

90
00:05:36.774 --> 00:05:38.799
The clerk must preserve the record.

91
00:05:38.799 --> 00:05:42.040
Conditions must reach the departments that enforce them.

92
00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.239
Appeal deadlines must be communicated.

93
00:05:44.239 --> 00:05:49.622
Mitigation monitoring, permit expiration, phasing, and compliance reviews must be calendared.

94
00:05:49.622 --> 00:05:54.251
A defensible hearing followed by careless administration still produces failure.

95
00:05:54.251 --> 00:05:56.508
Now run the Torrance capstone scenario.

96
00:05:56.508 --> 00:06:00.617
A mixed-use project near employment and retail seeks several approvals.

97
00:06:00.617 --> 00:06:02.990
State housing law limits some discretion.

98
00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.231
The environmental document identifies mitigated effects.

99
00:06:06.231 --> 00:06:13.118
Residents submit a late consultant letter disputing a traffic assumption and report unequal access to earlier outreach.

100
00:06:13.118 --> 00:06:15.317
One commissioner owns property nearby.

101
00:06:15.317 --> 00:06:17.806
Another toured the site with the applicant.

102
00:06:17.806 --> 00:06:19.195
First, pause the merits.

103
00:06:19.195 --> 00:06:23.824
The property interest goes to the city attorney for a current conflict analysis.

104
00:06:23.824 --> 00:06:29.438
The tour is disclosed with its substance, and counsel decides whether further response is needed.

105
00:06:29.438 --> 00:06:35.341
Staff identifies which approvals are legislative, adjudicative, ministerial, final, or recommendatory.

106
00:06:35.341 --> 00:06:37.656
The chair confirms notice and deadlines.

107
00:06:37.656 --> 00:06:40.190
Second, classify each substantive concern.

108
00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:45.982
The traffic letter may affect CEQA, a local circulation standard, or both, depending on content.

109
00:06:45.982 --> 00:06:51.352
Do not confuse level-of-service frustration with CEQA's vehicle-miles-traveled framework.

110
00:06:51.352 --> 00:06:59.678
The outreach concern may implicate adopted engagement commitments, statutory duties, environmental-justice analysis, or policy legitimacy.

111
00:06:59.678 --> 00:07:03.600
Determine what legal and policy decision each concern can affect.

112
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.772
Third, test the late traffic letter.

113
00:07:05.772 --> 00:07:07.462
Is the consultant qualified?

114
00:07:07.462 --> 00:07:11.383
Does the letter analyze the current project and correct baseline?

115
00:07:11.383 --> 00:07:16.994
Does it reveal a factual omission, disagree over method, or merely prefer a different policy?

116
00:07:16.994 --> 00:07:19.167
Can staff and the applicant respond?

117
00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:24.174
Does the issue require new analysis, a limited response, a condition, or no change?

118
00:07:24.174 --> 00:07:26.286
State the conclusion on the record.

119
00:07:26.286 --> 00:07:29.906
Fourth, evaluate equity without counting speakers as a vote.

120
00:07:29.906 --> 00:07:36.060
Review who was reached, barriers identified, affected groups, and whether missing information remains.

121
00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:40.284
If the process failed a legal requirement, cure it as counsel directs.

122
00:07:40.284 --> 00:07:50.058
If it met legal requirements but exposed a policy weakness, acknowledge it and improve future engagement without inventing unauthorized grounds for this decision.

123
00:07:50.058 --> 00:07:52.532
Fifth, deliberate on the actual findings.

124
00:07:52.532 --> 00:07:56.514
For every yes or no, cite record facts and explain the connection.

125
00:07:56.514 --> 00:08:06.047
If a concern cannot lawfully affect this entitlement, say where it belongs, perhaps in a code amendment, capital plan, budget, or future general-plan process.

126
00:08:06.047 --> 00:08:08.581
Redirecting an issue is not dismissing it.

127
00:08:08.581 --> 00:08:10.528
Here are three recall questions.

128
00:08:10.528 --> 00:08:11.319
Question one.

129
00:08:11.319 --> 00:08:13.813
What should the decision charter contain?

130
00:08:13.813 --> 00:08:14.239
Answer.

131
00:08:14.239 --> 00:08:20.566
The body, requested action, role, authority, findings, environmental action, appeal path, and deadlines.

132
00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:21.357
Question two.

133
00:08:21.357 --> 00:08:23.304
How should deliberation proceed?

134
00:08:23.304 --> 00:08:23.729
Answer.

135
00:08:23.729 --> 00:08:33.037
Finding by finding, with disputed evidence weighed, contrary evidence addressed, reasoning stated, and conditions tested for legality and administration.

136
00:08:33.037 --> 00:08:33.950
Question three.

137
00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.931
What makes the hearing defensible after the vote?

138
00:08:36.931 --> 00:08:37.357
Answer.

139
00:08:37.357 --> 00:08:45.570
A precise motion, accurate final documents, a preserved record, communicated appeal rights, and reliable implementation and monitoring.

140
00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.977
Your learner artifact is a complete mock-hearing binder.

141
00:08:48.977 --> 00:09:02.665
Include the decision charter, process audit, conflict and contact checklist, standards matrix, evidence index, speaker procedure, continuance test, condition checklist, model motion, and post-decision implementation calendar.

142
00:09:02.665 --> 00:09:05.524
Conduct a practice hearing with assigned roles.

143
00:09:05.524 --> 00:09:12.338
Record it, then score whether every ruling and finding can be understood by a listener who never saw the packet.

144
00:09:12.338 --> 00:09:24.680
This course finale independently explains themes in the American Planning Association's free Planning Officials Training and Institute for Local Government training and handbook resources.

145
00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:38.007
Governance concepts are adapted from MIT OpenCourseWare's Frameworks of Urban Governance and attributed under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 for noncommercial, share-alike use.

146
00:09:38.007 --> 00:09:55.141
For controlling guidance, consult current California law, authoritative state and regional materials, the Torrance Charter and Municipal Code, the current staff report, official City of Torrance procedures, and the Torrance city attorney as of the hearing date.
